the days are getting shorter the sun is  rising later and yet another summer has  passed without resolution to the small  boats crisis  first light and we spot a recently  abandoned dinghy the disregarded clothes  and a blanket are still wet  we counted at least 10 life jackets the  people who wore them have now moved on  but they've left behind communities  which whilst largely empathetic appear  exhausted divided and frustrated after  the uk committed nearly 200 million  pounds to the french over the last six  years to help break the cycle  mike golding has lived here for more  than 40 years and says it's become  normal to see migrants arriving on the  beaches  you've got to feel sympathy for them but  you've got to draw the line and  how many are you going to live into the  country and what you're going to do  about it to stop them the french are not  going to do anything  they want rid of them as much as we  don't want them are you kind of resigned  to the way things are yeah very much so  nowadays you just take it for granted  they're going to come  and that's it really nothing we can do  at this level but others can't contain  their anger and are turning their  frustrations directly onto the migrants  lawbreakers shouts one woman  they're coming in now to land  can you tell me why you're here  another local resident kim rai films as  she challenges new arrivals on the beach  at dungeness a few weeks ago was france  not a nice country to live in  it's organized it's organized crime it's  divisive as well  we went to see kim and her husband  graham who say they want to make it  clear they welcome what they call  genuine refugees but admit their  attitude to asylum seekers and in  particular economic migrants is hostile  young men  getting off a boat and strutting up the  shingle wearing nike trainers carrying  smartphones  i'm sorry but that's not a refugee  oh are you pleased to be here of course  we are if you lived through this every  day  if you were listening to the helicopters  going up the police sirens  and that's why i'm angry i feel very  protective of this community nobody  knows who those people are they don't  know who they are they don't know where  they've come from the longer it goes on  the greater the chance is that something  nasty will happen somebody coming up  that beach tooled up with ak-47s  that's not the way everyone views the  migrants  terry preston has lived on this coast  for most of his life it's very divisive  and it certainly divided the community  we need to treat these people with  compassion and justice and of course  there's got to be an immigration system  we can't in this small island take  everyone who comes but there's got to be  a compassionate way in which that's  dealt with  the government says it's determined to  tackle the rise in channel crossings but  without a significant shift in policies  they're likely to remain the backdrop to  the lives of those who live in these  coastal communities lisa holland sky  news kent
 

